pro.2018.10.105"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-31","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.trpro.2017.05.059"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-32","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-319-51168-9_52"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-33","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.3390\/su11226259"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-34","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.3141\/2143-20"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-35","unstructured":"Shuyo, N. (2010). Language Detection Library for Java. Retrieved from https:\/\/github.com\/shuyo\/language-detection\/"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-36","author":"D.Smiley","year":"2011","unstructured":"SmileyD.PughD. E. (2011). Apache Solr 3 Enterprise Search Server. Packt Publishing Ltd.","journal-title":"Apache Solr 3 Enterprise Search Server"},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-37","unstructured":"Socher, R., Perelygin, A., Wu, J., Chuang, J., Manning, C. D., Ng, A. Y., & Potts, C. (2013, October). Recursive deep models for semantic compositionality over a sentiment treebank. In Proceedings of the 2013 conference on empirical methods in natural language processing (pp. 1631-1642). Academic Press."},{"key":"978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059.-38","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.trc.2015.01.030"}],"container-title":["Research Anthology on Implementing Sentiment Analysis Across Multiple Disciplines"],"language":"ng","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.igi-global.com\/viewtitle.aspx?TitleId=308537","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2022,7,22]],"date-time":"2022-07-22T13:11:46Z","timestamp":1658495506000},"score":23.361029,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/services.igi-global.com\/resolvedoi\/resolve.aspx?doi=10.4018\/978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059"}},"subtitle":["Transport Domain Adaptation of Sentiment Labelled Data Set"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,6,10]]},"ISBN":["9781668463031","9781668463048"],"references-count":38,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/978-1-6684-6303-1.ch059","published":{"date-parts":[[2022,6,10]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,14]],"date-time":"2025-05-14T04:14:20Z","timestamp":1747196060848,"version":"3.40.5"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Science Research Society","issue":"42s","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["jisem"],"abstract":"<jats:p>Multimodal sentiment analysis faces significant challenges due to data scarcity and domain shift, which hinder model generalization across different datasets. To address these issues, we propose a Multimodal Domain Adaptation framework that combines advanced feature extraction techniques, attention-based fusion, and adversarial domain adaptation to enhance sentiment classification. Our approach builds on Domain-Adversarial Neural Networks (DANN) to facilitate knowledge transfer from a labeled source dataset to an unlabeled target dataset, reducing domain discrepancies while preserving sentiment prediction accuracy. Unlike existing methods that focus primarily on single-modality adaptation or basic feature alignment, our framework performs comprehensive cross-modal and cross-domain feature alignment to improve generalization. Specifically, we extract high-quality feature embeddings for both text and image modalities using state-of-the-art deep learning models. To bridge modal gaps, we integrate an attention-based fusion mechanism that prioritizes the most informative modality, ensuring optimal feature integration. Additionally, we employ a Gradient Reversal Layer (GRL) and a domain discriminator to reduce domain loss, enabling the model to learn domain-invariant representations. Our experimental results demonstrate that Adversarial Multimodal Sentiment Adaptation with Cross-Modal and Cross-Domain Alignment (AMSA-CMCDA) significantly enhance performance in sentiment classification across datasets. By effectively addressing both modality mismatch and domain shift, our approach proves its effectiveness in real-world sentiment analysis applications.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.52783\/jisem.v10i42s.7850","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,13]],"date-time":"2025-05-13T09:45:52Z","timestamp":1747129552000},"page":"25-35","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Adversarial Multimodal Sentiment Analysis with Cross-Modal and Cross-Domain Alignment"],"prefix":"10.52783","volume":"10","author":[{"family":"Vani Golagana,","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"30593","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,3]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information Systems Engineering and Management"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jisem-journal.com\/index.php\/journal\/article\/download\/7850\/3562","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jisem-journal.com\/index.php\/journal\/article\/download\/7850\/3562","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,13]],"date-time":"2025-05-13T09:45:52Z","timestamp":1747129552000},"score":23.2992,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/jisem-journal.com\/index.php\/journal\/article\/view\/7850"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,3]]},"references-count":0,"journal-issue":{"issue":"42s","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,3]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52783\/jisem.v10i42s.7850","ISSN":["2468-4376"],"issn-type":[{"value":"2468-4376","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,3]]}}],"items-per-page":30,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":"transparent lexical sentiment baselines cross-domain reviews"}}}